WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
If you own a local business, this is a

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:07.040
new way to add a new revenue stream, and

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.560
it's called Amazon Hub Delivery. Most of

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.600
you guys know I have had several

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:12.280
businesses in my life. Most notably, I

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:15.520
had an online swimmer brand that I

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:17.680
started back in 2018. So, let's be real

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.640
first hand because I know first hand

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.000
that most local businesses are not

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.580
running at 100% capacity every single

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.760
day, all day long. The truth is

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.720
[clears throat] that there are slow

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.320
hours, there's gaps between customers,

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.000
and there are days when the traffic just

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.680
isn't there. And during those times,

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.160
your staff is still on payroll, and your

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.400
overhead is still running. But, the

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:36.000
downside is, of course, there's nothing

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.920
actually generating revenue during those

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.520
moments. And that's the part that most

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:41.600
people don't think about. How much

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.120
unused capacity already exists within

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:45.360
the business. So, what if there is

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.240
actually a way to monetize that

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:49.125
downtime? Well, that's exactly where

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.720
Amazon Hub Delivery comes in. Now, let

00:00:49.125 --> 00:00:51.880
[clears throat] me guess, you've

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:54.000
probably never heard of this. So, let me

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.760
break it down super simply for you. It's

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.960
a program designed specifically for

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.000
local businesses to earn additional

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.720
revenue by delivering Amazon customer

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.200
packages in their area. And what I love

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.600
about this is that it's not actually

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.000
asking you to become something

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.960
completely different. It's totally

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.760
designed to fit within your existing

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:11.360
business. You're really just layering a

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.080
new revenue stream on top of what you

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.720
already do. Once you understand how it

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.920
actually works, it's a lot simpler than

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.000
you might think. [music] So, here's how

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.840
it works in practice. Every day, Amazon

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.680
drops off packages directly to your

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:23.400
business location. From there, you and

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:25.080
your existing staff actually take those

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.360
packages and deliver them locally. You

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:28.320
can do it during your normal business

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:30.160
hours, or really whatever fits into your

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.120
schedule. So, think about it like this.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.680
Let's say that you own a local coffee

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:35.840
shop. You've got a morning rush, but

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:37.120
let's say from 10:30 to 1:00, things

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.680
tend to slow down quite a bit. Instead

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.640
of your staff just waiting around for

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:42.680
the next wave of customers, one of your

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:45.320
employees could actually take a batch of

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.120
packages and complete deliveries during

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.960
that window. They are back in time for

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.520
the next rush, and now that downtime

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.600
actually generated additional revenue

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.040
for your business. The same concept can

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.880
apply to so many different business

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.680
models. It could be salons during gaps

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.680
between appointments, gyms during

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.320
off-peak hours, florists or boutiques

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:02.960
during slower parts of the day. Now,

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:04.360
those are just a few examples and if you

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:06.080
have a local business and you want some

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:07.680
ideas of how you could incorporate this,

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.320
comment below what your local business

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.320
is and I will help you brainstorm the

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.920
best ways to fit this into your existing

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.280
business model. I really actually think

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:15.720
this would have been so great for me

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.120
when I was running Boutine Bikini

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.080
because it really was a seasonal

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.440
business primarily during the spring and

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.120
summer months. I would have a really

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:23.880
strong busy season from, let's say,

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:25.800
February through July, but the rest of

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.280
the year was a lot slower. So, this

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.480
would have been a perfect time for me to

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.760
take advantage of a program like this.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.040
Remember, you're not actually changing

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:34.080
anything about your business, you're

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.520
just leveraging the natural downtime

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.600
that already exists. So, you might be

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.760
thinking, "Okay, well, this sounds like

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.400
a good idea, but how can you actually

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.040
make money from this?" So, next let's

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.920
talk about how the actual revenue side

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:45.840
works. You actually get paid for every

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.600
single package that you deliver. So,

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:49.440
instead of relying on customers to walk

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:51.480
through your door, you now have a second

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.200
stream of revenue that is tied to

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:54.840
something that you can actively control.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.800
It's all about using downtime versus

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.480
losing downtime. And that's a big shift

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.480
because now your slower hours aren't

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.360
just dead time, they're actually

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.160
opportunities. And over time, this can

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.680
really add up in a consistent and

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.959
meaningful way. And what makes this even

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.120
more appealing is that you actually

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:10.720
don't need much to get started. In fact,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.519
I think that one of the biggest reasons

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.000
this opportunity really stands out to me

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:16.880
is because it is so easy to get started.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:18.600
There's no upfront cost and there are no

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.040
long-term contracts locking you into

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.400
anything. So, you're not putting a ton

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.959
of capital on the line, you're not

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.680
making some huge commitment. You're

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:26.760
essentially just testing a new revenue

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.360
stream with very little downside. And

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:30.200
honestly, anybody that has run a

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.720
business before knows that that is

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.239
pretty rare when it comes to adding

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.519
something new to your business model.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.080
So, [clears throat] let's talk about how

